[h1]1.1.0[/h1]

Intercolony 1.1.0 adds production automation, agreements that can look after routine work, a real
procurement workflow, delivery destinations, a five-day Business view, and a deeper hiring market.

[hr][/hr]
[h1]Produce[/h1]

[list]
[*][b]Produce[/b] can be marked on minifiable furniture, blueprints and frames. Let the colony
repeat the build cycle in the same place, either indefinitely or until it has enough of the good.
[*][b]Pause production[/b] and [b]Stop production[/b] are different actions. All three commands
can be dragged over an area from [b]Architect → Orders[/b].
[*]Cancelling a Produce blueprint stops its loop instead of bringing it back.
[/list]

[h1]Agreements look after themselves[/h1]

[list]
[*]A selling agreement can ready a buyer-pickup cycle when its goods are available. Routine
automatic success no longer adds an [b]Order ready[/b] letter.
[*]A procurement cycle can wait for silver and try again instead of failing immediately.
[*]Employees can auto-renew when the worker offers another term. [b]Auto-renew[/b] is visible and
clickable on the employee card; the other contract actions are in its actions menu.
[*]New recurring agreements start automated, while agreements already in your save keep their
existing choices. Contract rows open when they need attention and stay compact otherwise.
[/list]

[h1]Procurement[/h1]

[list]
[*][b]Procurement → Contracts[/b] is now a real screen. Propose a standing purchase, preview its
terms before sending, follow the answer, and manage live or concluded agreements.
[*]Proposal and contract rows show the term, delivery arrangement, per-unit price and per-cycle
price. A purchase agreement has to be earned through reputation and completed purchases from that
settlement, and furniture can be covered by an agreement.
[*]Supplier replies arrive over time, with nearer suppliers answering sooner. Proposal screens show
the likely acceptance band before you commit.
[/list]

[h1]Deliveries and Business[/h1]

[list]
[*]Mark a stockpile or shelf [b]Receive deliveries[/b] and supplier goods will prefer a marked
destination that accepts them and has room.
[*][b]Business[/b] now shows five days of committed revenue, expenses and net cash flow.
[*]See what the colony committed to make beside what it actually completed over the last five days.
Direct material costs and the labour of employees who can make the good now feed the margin.
[/list]

[h1]Hiring[/h1]

[list]
[*]Job postings ask for a requirement and report the going rate. A hired applicant is paid their
own ask, and a bad employer attracts worse applicants rather than merely fewer.
[*]An employee's weapons and apparel are shown as an [b]Equipment bond[/b] at hire. The bond is
replacement value plus 10%, is included in [b]Due at hire[/b], and returns item by item for gear the
worker still carries when employment ends.
[*][b]Emergency dispatch[/b] offers the nearest half of the market, a 4× wage premium, and a
journey shortened to one third with a one-day minimum.
[/list]

[h1]Relationships and settings[/h1]

[list]
[*]Commercial standing can improve faction goodwill over time, stopping below alliance. The
[b]Relations[/b] row explains why the pressure is or is not active.
[*]After enough ordinary employment, the worker's observed experience can improve or reduce
goodwill with the settlement that sent them. The departure letter explains the result.
[*]Nine new settings cover commercial goodwill, employment experience and supplier-response speed.
[/list]

[h1]Fixes[/h1]

[list]
[*]Wage displays now separate the worker's ask from the colony's charge, and direct hires are no
longer quoted one amount and charged another.
[*]Profitability uses a made good's materials, equipment bonds include quality, and ordinary hiring
travel is limited to one to twenty days.
[*]Selling and supplier markets no longer rebuild every row every frame, and [b]Find Buyer[/b]
searches storage rather than the whole map.
[*]A newly dead employee is no longer discarded from the grave that contains them during cleanup.
Existing empty graves are not repaired automatically.
[/list]

[h1]New defaults[/h1]

Fresh installs now start with quieter [b]Minimal[/b] letters, a [b]0.25-day[/b] market refresh,
[b]50[/b] active opportunities, and cooked meals and stone blocks enabled for selling. Commercial
goodwill is faster but much more limited by default: [b]+2 every 7 days[/b], requiring [b]85[/b]
commercial reputation and stopping at [b]15[/b] goodwill. Employment goodwill uses four observed
days and an impact of [b]±2[/b].

[b]Existing players keep their saved settings; nothing is reset.[/b]

[h1]Saves[/h1]

A 1.0 save from [b]schema 56[/b] loads and upgrades to save format [b]58[/b]. The upgrade adds
per-agreement auto-renew and auto-ready flags with existing values off, then adds production
tracking with no invented history. Existing agreements are not silently enabled.

[h1]Known limits[/h1]

The new Produce controls, automatic agreements, receiving destinations, Business view, supplier
timing, contract layout, equipment bonds and emergency hiring still need human play checks; they
are not presented here as play-verified.

[list]
[*]A Produce loop can stall if minified furniture has nowhere to be hauled.
[*]A pre-existing delivery issue can complete a partly placed supplier delivery while charging the
full order.
[*]There is no automatic recurring caravan or animal dispatch, reverse player-supplied labour
market, equipment-tier request, or transport-pod arrival. Equipment bonds and emergency dispatch
are the shipped portions of those larger ideas.
[*]Automatic ready is for buyer-pickup cycles; seller-delivery agreements still need a hand-formed
caravan.
[*]The new grave save/load guard still needs a clean real-play check; existing empty graves are not
repaired automatically.
[/list]

Problems to the [url=https://github.com/miannoni/Intercolony/issues]GitHub issue tracker[/url], please,
with your mod list and [b]Player.log[/b].
